WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:03.940
Where can you go to find all the best horror
movies you've never heard of?

00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:05.340
One word: Netflix.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:09.280
The streaming service is packed with zombies
and vampires and hauntings that have flown

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:10.560
under the radar too long.

00:00:10.860 --> 00:00:15.620
Here are the most underrated, scream-worthy,
stream-able films currently available on Netflix.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:17.020
​Midnight Meat Train

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:21.780
Before he gave his voice to a bionically-enhanced
space raccoon, Bradley Cooper was the star

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:25.250
of this incredibly creepy, bizarrely overlooked
New York City horror story.

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:29.250
Midnight Meat Train tells the story of Leon,
a photographer who accidentally uncovers the

00:00:29.250 --> 00:00:33.440
existence of a murderous butcher who's using
the New York City subway as his own personal

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:33.940
slaughterhouse.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:38.040
Needless to say, things get gory — and the
film goes for broke in its final scene with

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:39.980
a seriously twisted surprise ending.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:43.880
Sadly, a delayed and limited theatrical release
kept Midnight Meat Train from getting the

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:48.400
attention it deserved, especially considering
Cooper's star power and the movie's production

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:51.620
values—but that just makes it one of Netflix's
most hidden gems.

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.440
​Train to Busan

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.650
The only thing scarier than a roving pack
of zombies is being trapped with them in a

00:00:56.650 --> 00:01:00.399
claustrophobic train that's flying through
the Korean countryside at a hundred miles

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:00.900
an hour.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:05.360
Enter Train to Busan, a fabulous under-the-radar
horror film that milks its unique setting

00:01:05.369 --> 00:01:06.369
for all it's worth.

00:01:06.369 --> 00:01:10.340
The titular train is barreling through a world
overrun by the undead, bound for a southern

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:13.939
resort city that's rumored to be the last
safe stronghold for humanity.

00:01:13.939 --> 00:01:18.100
Train to Busan was a hit at the Cannes Film
Festival in 2016 and broke box office records

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:22.999
in Korea, but it was almost completely overlooked
by American audiences and critics; fortunately,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.120
it's ripe for discovery as a new addition
to Netflix's horror library.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:27.220
The Pact

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.860
Despite a promising release at Sundance in
2012, The Pact never found the audience it

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:36.139
deserved during its original, limited theatrical
run—but this smart, spooky horror flick

00:01:36.139 --> 00:01:38.009
is a perfect pick for streaming.

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:41.709
Starring Caity Lotz—who you might know as
Arrow's original ass-kicking Canary—The

00:01:41.709 --> 00:01:45.149
Pact tells the story of a young woman with
a troubled past whose mother has died and

00:01:45.149 --> 00:01:49.039
whose sister has gone missing…and whose
house may or may not have a few haunted skeletons

00:01:49.039 --> 00:01:50.840
hanging around in its closets.

00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:54.869
Director Nicholas McCarthy wrings every last
iota of suspense from his miniscule budget,

00:01:54.869 --> 00:01:58.860
and Lotz's performance anchors what is ultimately
a quiet but intensely creepy film.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:00.340
We Are Still Here

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:05.340
This debut film from director Ted Geoghegan
is a modern-day throwback to classic horror.

00:02:05.340 --> 00:02:08.311
It tells the story of a couple mourning the
death of their son who move to the country

00:02:08.311 --> 00:02:12.330
in search of a fresh start—only to find
themselves haunted by an unspeakable evil.

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:16.780
We Are Still Here premiered at South by Southwest
in 2015 and was considered by many critics

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.250
to be one of the best horror films of the
year, with an inventive plot and just the

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.170
right amount of artfully-executed gore.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:24.200
Since then, though, it's been tragically overlooked.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.020
​V/H/S/2

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:30.580
The original horror-anthology film V/H/S has
gotten a decent amount of attention for its

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:34.910
inventive, bite-size tales of terror—because
why take two hours for a slow-burn frightfest

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:38.110
when you can pack multiple horror stories
into the same amount of time?

00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:42.060
V/H/S was successful enough to spawn a sequel,
which sticks to the original format but offers

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.840
up an entirely fresh crop of scares.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.680
Like its predecessor, V/H/S/2 includes something
for everyone, from zombies to aliens to haunted

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:49.360
body parts.

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:50.940
Stake Land

00:02:51.640 --> 00:02:55.510
This underrated apocalypse film tells the
story of an orphaned teenage boy named Martin

00:02:55.510 --> 00:02:58.830
living in a world where vampires have overrun
every human stronghold.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:02.850
With the help of a mysterious vampire-hunter
known only as Mister, Martin makes his way

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:07.510
across America in search of a safe haven,
dodging killers both human and supernatural.

00:03:07.510 --> 00:03:11.290
Making the most of a tiny budget, Stake Land
is a best-of-both-worlds mashup between the

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:15.710
apocalyptic horrors of The Walking Dead and
the action of Blade: a smart and compelling

00:03:15.710 --> 00:03:20.070
survival drama full of interesting characters,
thoughtful social commentary, and, of course,

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:21.340
bloodsucking monsters.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.660
Thanks for watching!

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.620
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.940
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

